### 5. Engage worktree (loop top — once, before any iter)

If `harness.yaml.worktree.enabled` is on, create one worktree
that wraps the **entire loop**. Per-loop (not per-iter) — improve and
feature mode both default to one squash-merge at convergence. Per-iter
worktree would explode commit count.

{% if is_codex %}
```
Bash("uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} hm worktree create execute \"$(pwd)\" --claude-session-id \"$HM_SESSION_ID\"")
```

If the command fails (non-zero exit or git error):
- Warn the user: "Worktree creation failed — proceeding in-place (no isolation)."
- Set `<WT>` = the current working directory. Continue.
{% else %}
```bash
!uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} hm worktree create execute "$(pwd)" --claude-session-id "$HM_SESSION_ID"
```
{% endif %}

Read **all non-empty output lines** the command prints. Three cases:

- **Empty output** → `worktree.enabled` is off. No isolation; operate in
  `cwd`. Skip the finalize step at the end.
- **One absolute path** like `/path/to/repo/.worktrees/execute-20260507T0010Z`
  → single-repo isolation. **Treat that exact string as `<WT>` for every
  subsequent operation in this loop**: every Read/Write/Edit call, every
  {% if is_codex %}`Bash("cd <WT> && ...")` for tests/lints{% else %}`cd` for tests/lints{% endif %}, every workflow-command invocation. Do NOT use a
  shell variable{% if not is_codex %} — each `!` block is a fresh subshell{% endif %}.
- **Multiple lines** → multi-repo isolation. Line 1 = primary repo worktree
  (`<WT>`). Lines 2+ = sibling repo worktrees (`<WT-sibling-N>`). Use `<WT>`
  for primary-repo operations and `<WT-sibling-N>` for sibling-repo edits.

**Verify the worktree before using it (anti-drift gate — do NOT skip).** `verify`
confirms the path is a real **linked** git worktree root — it does NOT parse the
name (a path with no uuid segment or a round timestamp like
`execute-20260531T120000Z` is a human tell-tale of drift; the real format is
`execute-<uuid12>-<timestamp>`, but the gate's actual check is structural, not
textual). Run `verify` on the exact path you just read. **Multi-repo: run
`verify` on EVERY printed line** — `<WT>` and each `<WT-sibling-N>` — and HALT
if any one fails:

{% if is_codex %}
```
Bash("uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} hm worktree verify <WT>")
```
{% else %}
```bash
!uv run --with {{ harness_maker_src_path }} hm worktree verify <WT>
```
{% endif %}

- **Exit 0** (re-prints `<WT>`) → the worktree is real. Proceed.
- **Non-zero** → the path is phantom, not a worktree, a subdirectory of one, or
  the main repo root (drift). **HALT the loop now**:
  do NOT write `.current-iter`, do NOT dispatch any stage, do NOT issue further
  calls. Re-run `worktree create`, read its exact printed path, and retry
  `verify`. Never proceed on an unverified path.

> ⚠️ **Never batch worktree-dependent operations in one parallel tool-call
> turn.** `create` → `verify` → `.current-iter` marker must run **serially**,
> each confirmed before the next. If you fire the marker write, receipt writes,
> and stage sub-agent dispatches as parallel calls in a single turn and the
> path is wrong, one `cd <WT>` error cancels the entire batch (every sibling
> shows `Cancelled: parallel tool call … errored`). Serialize the
> create→verify→marker prelude; only parallelize work already inside a
> verified `<WT>`.

{%- else -%}
### 5. Isolation is off (`worktree.enabled: false`)

No worktree is created and none is finalized at loop close. Every path below that
would name a worktree names this project's root instead — run the loop where you
already are, and do not invent a `.worktrees/` path.

One consequence to hold: the loop's deliverables and code changes accumulate
**uncommitted on the current branch** until wrapup commits them. That is the cost of
`worktree.enabled: false`, not a failure — but it means a mid-loop `git status` is
expected to be dirty, and an unrelated concurrent session shares that working tree.

{% if not is_codex %}The Stop-hook loop marker is still written (`.hm-loop-active` at
the project root) so `/hm:loop` still self-gates; without isolation it is the
session-blind legacy marker, so **parallel loops in this project are NOT isolated**.
{% endif %}
{%- endif %}

#### 5.1 Stop-hook guard (both isolation modes)

After confirming the worktree path (or deciding to operate in cwd), **activate
the Stop-hook guard — session-scoped**. The guard now keys on THIS Claude
session, so parallel loops in other sessions are never affected
(PLAN-loop-marker-session-scoping):

{% if wt_on %}- **Normal path (worktree isolation engaged AND `$HM_SESSION_ID` is set):**
  `worktree create --claude-session-id "$HM_SESSION_ID"` already wrote this
  session's per-session marker, whose content header carries your `session_id`.
  The Stop-hook matches that header against your own `session_id`, so the loop is
  already guarded. **Do NOT touch the global `.hm-loop-active`** — it is
  session-blind and would block other sessions' termination.
{% endif %}
- **Degraded path (`$HM_SESSION_ID` empty — no SessionStart env-file, or
  Cursor/Codex — OR no isolation):** there is no session-scoped marker, so fall
  back to the global marker. **Two distinct symptoms** (PLAN-fleet-10-20-parallel-safety
  Phase 0 spike): in **Claude Code** with an empty `$HM_SESSION_ID` (a SessionStart-hook
  failure — a SHELL read, so empty only if the hook truly did not fire) the Stop-hook
  still has your real `session_id` from stdin, so it
  cannot content-match the empty marker and **this loop self-stops after one
  iteration** — peers are NOT affected. In **Cursor/Codex or no-isolation** the
  global marker is the only guard and **parallel loops are NOT isolated**. The
  guard warns with the symptom that actually applies:

{% if wt_on %}Substitute `<WT>` with the literal worktree path from the create step.{% else %}Isolation is off, so the first test is already true and the global marker is always written — that is the only guard available without per-worktree markers.{% endif %} The guard touches the global marker when EITHER
`$HM_SESSION_ID` is empty (no content header to match) OR there is no isolation
({{ WTP }} equals your cwd → no per-session content marker exists) — both are the
degraded path; otherwise the per-session content marker already guards you:

{% if is_codex %}
```
